LDLOAD(8)            UNIX Programmer's Manual            LDLOAD(8)



NAME
     ldload - make shared libraries resident

SYNOPSIS
     /sbin/ldload [conf]

DESCRIPTION
     Ldload makes libc resident, then each library the file conf
     lists, in its order -- /etc/ld.so.conf when conf is not
     given.  Each one it loads is pinned: it stays resident until
     the machine restarts.  Every other library is loaded when a
     program that needs it first runs, and stays cached while the
     bank pool can spare it.  /etc/rc runs ldload after modload(8)
     has loaded ld.mod, the registry ldload fills.

     The file holds one library a line, its name and its major
     version (`libtermlib 1'); `#' starts a comment.  A missing or
     empty file is not an error.  Cores stack in the order
     libraries load, so a library must be listed after every
     library it needs: one listed above a library it needs is
     refused, naming both, and the rest still load.  A library
     that a listed one needs and that is not listed loads first,
     unpinned.

     A library is /lib/name.so.major.minor, the highest minor of
     its major.

     Ldload prints a line for each library -- its version, banks,
     core base, and the time its load took, or `(resident)' when
     it already was -- and then how many it loaded and how many
     were resident.  It exits 2 if libc could not be made
     resident, 1 if any other library failed, and 0 otherwise.

FILES
     /etc/ld.so.conf    libraries resident from boot
     /lib/*.so.*.*      the shared libraries

SEE ALSO
     modload(8)

BUGS
     A library a program loaded before ldload ran, and that the
     file lists, stays unpinned.

     A library whose needed library changed since it was linked is
     refused until it is relinked.

     Any user can make an installed library resident, bounded by
     the banks its file needs.



